`})}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["That ",n(e.code,{children:"dyn"})," in ",n(e.code,{children:"dyn-prefill-worker-id"})," is ",n(e.a,{href:"https://www.baseten.co/blog/how-baseten-achieved-2x-faster-inference-with-nvidia-dynamo/",children:"NVIDIA Dynamo"}),". Baseten has written whole blog posts about running Dynamo's KV-aware router. Their ",n(e.a,{href:"https://docs.baseten.co/engines/bis-llm/advanced-features",children:"BIS-LLM engine docs"})," explain how their stateful router tokenizes each incoming prompt, checks a live index of every worker's KV cache blocks, and sends the request to the worker with the most cached tokens (balanced against queue depth)."]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["If Baseten already has a router that knows which GPU holds which KV blocks, why do their ",n(e.a,{href:"https://docs.baseten.co/inference/model-apis/pricing-and-limits#session-affinity",children:"Model API docs"})," still tell you to send ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"}),"? Why can't the provider just figure out that Turn 2 belongs with Turn 1 by looking at the prompt?"]}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"Let's try to design content-based routing ourselves and watch where it breaks."}),`
`,t(e.h3,{id:"attempt-1-hash-the-first-256-tokens-the-openai-way",children:[n(e.a,{"aria-hidden":"true",tabIndex:"-1",href:"#attempt-1-hash-the-first-256-tokens-the-openai-way",children:n(e.span,{className:"icon icon-link"})}),"Attempt 1: Hash the first 256 tokens (the OpenAI way)"]}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"OpenAI doesn't ask you for a session header on basic calls. Their router hashes the opening prefix of the prompt, typically the first 256 tokens, scoped to your organization."}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"When it's just you on your personal API key, that works great. Turn 1, Turn 2, and Turn 20 all start with the same system prompt, so they hash to the same machine."}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"Now put 500 engineers behind a company gateway that authenticates to the provider with one shared enterprise API key. At 9 AM, 50 engineers fire up Pi or Claude Code. Every single one of those sessions starts with the exact same 256-token system prompt."}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"If your router only hashes the first 256 tokens:"}),`
`,t(e.ol,{children:[`
`,n(e.li,{children:"All 50 engineers hash to the exact same machine."}),`
`,n(e.li,{children:"That machine saturates. On OpenAI, once a prefix crosses roughly 15 requests per minute, the router spills excess traffic onto secondary machines."}),`
`,n(e.li,{children:"Because the router is only looking at the first 256 tokens (which are identical for all 50 engineers), Engineer A's Turn 2 lands on Machine 2 while Engineer B's Turn 2 lands on Machine 1. Everyone's conversation tail misses."}),`
`]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["That spillover problem is why OpenAI added ",n(e.code,{children:"prompt_cache_key"})," to their API. It mixes a caller-supplied session ID into the prefix hash so 50 sessions sharing a system prompt spread across the fleet while each individual session stays sticky."]}),`
`,t(e.h3,{id:"attempt-2-hash-the-system-prompt-plus-the-first-user-message-messages2",children:[n(e.a,{"aria-hidden":"true",tabIndex:"-1",href:"#attempt-2-hash-the-system-prompt-plus-the-first-user-message-messages2",children:n(e.span,{className:"icon icon-link"})}),"Attempt 2: Hash the system prompt plus the first user message (",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"}),")"]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["Okay, hashing ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[0]"})," collides because everyone shares the same system prompt. What if the gateway hashes ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[0]"})," ",n(e.em,{children:"plus"})," ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[1]"})," (the user's opening question in the thread)?"]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:[n(e.a,{href:"https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/best-practices/prompt-caching",children:"OpenRouter"})," actually uses this as a fallback when you don't send an explicit ",n(e.code,{children:"session_id"})," or ",n(e.code,{children:"prompt_cache_key"}),". Two people rarely type the exact same first question, and in a normal chat thread ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"})," stays fixed from Turn 1 to Turn 50."]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["In agent workloads, ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"})," still blows up in three places:"]}),`
`,t(e.ul,{children:[`
`,t(e.li,{children:[n(e.strong,{children:"Sliding-window history truncation:"})," A lot of chat backends and LangGraph agents cap context by slicing the last ",n(e.code,{children:"N"})," messages (",n(e.code,{children:"messages[-10:]"})," or ",n(e.code,{children:'trim_messages(..., strategy="last")'}),"). The moment your session hits Turn 11, Turn 1 gets dropped. Now ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[1]"})," is different on every single request. Not only does that scramble a ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"})," routing hash, shifting the array left by one turn invalidates the entire KV prefix of the remaining history on every turn (dropping history cache hits to 0% unless you evict in chunks of 5 or 10 turns at a time)."]}),`
`,t(e.li,{children:[n(e.strong,{children:"Mutated system prompts:"})," Agents love stuffing live state into ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[0]"}),": current workflow step, progress percentage, timestamps, or freshly retrieved RAG chunks. When ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[0]"})," changes per turn, the hash changes per turn. (Fun side effect on OpenAI models: OpenAI serializes ",n(e.code,{children:"tools"})," ",n(e.em,{children:"before"})," ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[0]"}),". When ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[0]"})," mutates, your tool schemas still hit cache at ~20%. If your dashboard only checks ",n(e.code,{children:"cached_tokens > 0"}),", everything looks fine while 100% of your system prompt and history miss.)"]}),`
`,t(e.li,{children:[n(e.strong,{children:"Slash commands and subagents:"})," Automated PR-review bots and ",n(e.code,{children:"/review"})," slash commands start every run with identical ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"})," text, putting you right back in the hot-spot bucket. Meanwhile, spawned subagents start with a fresh ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"})," that doesn't match their parent."]}),`
`]}),`
`,t(e.h3,{id:"why-baseten-fireworks-and-cloudflare-split-routing-into-two-tiers",children:[n(e.a,{"aria-hidden":"true",tabIndex:"-1",href:"#why-baseten-fireworks-and-cloudflare-split-routing-into-two-tiers",children:n(e.span,{className:"icon icon-link"})}),"Why Baseten, Fireworks, and Cloudflare split routing into two tiers"]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["Once you see all the ways content hashing goes sideways, the design that Baseten, ",n(e.a,{href:"https://docs.fireworks.ai/guides/prompt-caching",children:"Fireworks AI"}),", and ",n(e.a,{href:"https://blog.cloudflare.com/high-performance-llms/",children:"Cloudflare Workers AI"})," converged on makes a lot more sense. Cloudflare summed it up in one line: ",t(e.em,{children:['"While we have KV-aware routing internally, we also rely on clients sending the ',n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"}),' in order to be explicit about prompt caching."']})]}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"Putting Baseten's Model API docs next to their BIS-LLM docs reveals a two-tier setup:"}),`
`,n("img",{src:h,alt:"Diagram of two-tier routing showing Tier 1 L7 Edge Router using x-session-affinity to pick a Replica unit, and Tier 2 Dynamo KV Router inside Replica 1 tokenizing the prompt to pick the warm GPU worker",width:1200,height:378,loading:"lazy"}),`
`,t(e.ol,{children:[`
`,t(e.li,{children:[n(e.strong,{children:"Tier 1 (Outer L7 load balancer across replicas):"})," Popular models scale across multiple independent replication units. At the outer HTTP edge, you do not want your load balancer parsing and tokenizing a 100k-token JSON body before it can pick a backend cluster. Tier 1 hashes ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"})," in constant time and pins your session to Replica 1."]}),`
`,t(e.li,{children:[n(e.strong,{children:"Tier 2 (Stateful KV-aware router inside the replica):"})," Once your request is inside Replica 1, that replica's Dynamo KV router tokenizes the prompt, checks its local worker pool's KV block index, and picks the warm GPU worker (spilling over to a sibling worker in the replica if the queue is full)."]}),`
`]}),`
`,n(e.p,{children:"That two-tier split also explains why my unpinned sessions were getting 31% to 55% cache hits instead of 0%. Whenever two turns happened to land on the same replica by luck, the inner KV router found the warm worker. Whenever the outer L7 balancer sent a turn to a different replica, that replica had an isolated KV pool and missed."}),`
`,t(e.h2,{id:"can-the-litellm-gateway-just-inject-the-header-for-us",children:[n(e.a,{"aria-hidden":"true",tabIndex:"-1",href:"#can-the-litellm-gateway-just-inject-the-header-for-us",children:n(e.span,{className:"icon icon-link"})}),"Can the LiteLLM gateway just inject the header for us?"]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["If the upstream provider wants ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"}),", you might wonder why the company LiteLLM proxy doesn't generate one on the fly."]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["I went through the ",n(e.code,{children:"BerriAI/litellm"})," source code to see what stock LiteLLM actually supports via ",n(e.code,{children:"config.yaml"})," today:"]}),`
`,t(e.ul,{children:[`
`,t(e.li,{children:[t(e.strong,{children:["Forwarding an existing session ID (",n(e.a,{href:"https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/pull/41033",children:"PR #41033"}),"):"]})," LiteLLM has a ",n(e.code,{children:"provider_affinity_header: x-session-affinity"})," setting for model deployments. If the caller sends ",n(e.code,{children:"x-litellm-session-id"})," or ",n(e.code,{children:"metadata.session_id"}),", LiteLLM copies it into ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"}),". If the caller sends neither, LiteLLM sends no header at all (",n(e.a,{href:"https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/issues/34766",children:"Issue #34766"})," is the open feature request to add OpenRouter's ",n(e.code,{children:"messages[:2]"})," fallback)."]}),`
`,t(e.li,{children:[n(e.strong,{children:"Proxy-level deployment pinning:"})," LiteLLM also has a router flag (",n(e.code,{children:"optional_pre_call_checks: [session_affinity, deployment_affinity]"}),") to pin a session to one deployment inside a multi-provider model group. Turning that on globally has some nasty sharp edges right now: if a pinned deployment dies, in-request retries keep hammering the dead deployment until cooldown triggers (",n(e.a,{href:"https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/issues/47482",children:"Issue #47482"}),"), and if a pinned deployment is saturated at its concurrency limit while a sibling sits idle, the pin refuses to yield and returns ",n(e.code,{children:"429"})," (",n(e.a,{href:"https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/issues/45868",children:"Issue #45868"}),")."]}),`
`]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["Passing ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"})," from the client through to Baseten avoids those LiteLLM router bugs when a model maps to a single Baseten deployment, because Baseten's outer load balancer treats the header as a soft hint and routes around dead replicas automatically. The catch is that header pass-through won't help if your LiteLLM model group load-balances the same model slug across two completely different providers (say, splitting traffic between Baseten and Cognition). And since ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"})," only controls routing, pausing for ten minutes on a busy pool still gives the worker plenty of time to evict your KV blocks from VRAM."]}),`
`,t(e.h2,{id:"one-last-trap-langgraphs-thread_id-never-leaves-your-database",children:[n(e.a,{"aria-hidden":"true",tabIndex:"-1",href:"#one-last-trap-langgraphs-thread_id-never-leaves-your-database",children:n(e.span,{className:"icon icon-link"})}),"One last trap: LangGraph's ",n(e.code,{children:"thread_id"})," never leaves your database"]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["Even after you know you need ",n(e.code,{children:"x-session-affinity"}),", agent frameworks make it easy to think you're sending a session ID when you aren't."]}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:["In LangGraph, every multi-turn tutorial has you pass a ",n(e.code,{children:"thread_id"})," in ",n(e.code,{children:"RunnableConfig"}),":"]}),`
